WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:02.770
INTRO:
In the past 2 years, the money supply has

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:08.380
increased from $15.5 trillion to $21.8 trillion.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:13.190
This has caused asset valuations to go parabolic
and inflation to go through the roof.

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:18.940
We’re seeing CPI readings come in at over
8% and gas prices in some parts of the country

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:22.110
have soared past $6 per gallon.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:26.570
Despite all these red flags, for the longest
time, the Federal Reserve was insisting that

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:32.220
inflation was simply transitory, and that
it was just a matter of time until prices

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.220
stabilized.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:37.489
While the market was somewhat skeptical of
this idea, it gave the fed some credit until

00:00:37.489 --> 00:00:41.390
the fed rugpulled everyone and became extremely
hawkish.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:45.360
Eversince then, the stock market has gotten
crushed with many mega tech companies falling

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.100
50, 60, 70% from their all time highs.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.879
Meanwhile, the housing market is starting
to show its first signs of weakness with new

00:00:52.879 --> 00:00:55.480
home sales dropping off substantially.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.220
Considering all this, you would expect for
the dollar to currently be performing a cliff

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.300
dive manuever, and this is what’s happening
from a nominal perspective.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:08.340
However, from a relative perspective, the
dollar is actually skyrocketing.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.020
Wait wait wait wait, what am I even talking
about.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:14.540
Well, I’m talking about the dollar index
or ticker symbol DXY.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:18.870
If you’re not familiar with the dollar index,
it seeks to measure the strength of the US

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.780
dollar relative to a basket of foreign currencies.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.360
And over the past 12 months, the DXY has mooned
to multi year highs.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.660
And if you really want to get technical with
it, you’ll see that the DXY has actually

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.740
reached a slightly higher level than the peaks
of the past several years.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:39.510
In fact, the last time the DXY was this higher
than this way back in December of 2002 or

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:41.530
nearly 20 years ago.

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:45.210
And if you don’t believe this index, then
just take a look at foreign conversion rates.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:49.000
The Euro, for example, has fallen to multi
years lows against the dollar, and it’s

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.310
nearly at 1 dollar per euro.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:55.900
Similarly, the British pound is also testing
multi year lows against the dollar, and the

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:59.990
last time the pound was sizeably weaker than
this was way back in 1985.

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:05.070
Clearly, while the US dollar is getting crushed
in terms of buying power, it’s actually

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:09.269
gaining dominance on the world currencies,
so what in the world is going on with the

00:02:09.269 --> 00:02:11.209
dollar?

00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:16.599
SUPPLY &amp; DEMAND:
Starting with the biggest culprit, we have

00:02:16.599 --> 00:02:17.989
supply and demand.

00:02:17.989 --> 00:02:22.549
Though we often like to think of currencies
as a stable medium of exchange during regular

00:02:22.549 --> 00:02:26.670
times, currencies are actually subject to
all of the same supply and demand characteristics

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.170
that affect something like the stock market.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:32.129
Currencies simply have extremely low volatility
during regular times.

00:02:32.129 --> 00:02:35.689
However, with ground breaking events like
printing trillions of dollars followed up

00:02:35.689 --> 00:02:40.139
by suddenly raising interest rates, it’s
not surprising that even currencies are experiencing

00:02:40.139 --> 00:02:42.019
abnormally high volatility.

00:02:42.019 --> 00:02:47.980
Also, it’s not just the US who has been
engaging in such activities either.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:51.629
Most of the world’s central banks have engaged
in very similar monetary polcies.

00:02:51.629 --> 00:02:54.870
Take the European Central bank or the ECB
for example.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:58.010
Since the pandemic started, they have also
pumped trillions of dollars euros into the

00:02:58.010 --> 00:02:59.010
market.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:03.569
Now, this isn’t quite as high as the US
in terms of dollars, but the thing to note

00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:07.420
is that relative to economic metrics, the
money printing of other central banks are

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.889
just as high if not higher.

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:15.049
For example, the total GDP of all the Europeon
Union countries that use the Euro is $12.23

00:03:15.049 --> 00:03:16.049
trillion.

00:03:16.049 --> 00:03:21.689
While this is an extremely respectable figure,
it’s only about 60% of the United State’s

00:03:21.689 --> 00:03:23.450
$20.94 trillion GDP.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:28.110
Now, this isn’t an exact science by any
means, but this leads us to believe the the

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:33.459
ECB cannot print more than 60% of the dollars
printed by the US if they want to maintain

00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:35.819
the exchange rate between the two currencies.

00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:38.530
This same principle applies to smaller economies
as well.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:45.040
The UK, for example, has a GDP of $2.7 trillion
which a little more than an eighth of the

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:46.260
US.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:50.840
Given that the US has increased their money
supply by about $6.3 trillion, this means

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:54.669
that the UK could increase their money supply
by about $800 billion.

00:03:54.669 --> 00:03:58.609
However, the UK has surpassed this threshold
by quite a bit.

00:03:58.609 --> 00:04:00.730
So far, the UK has printed 895 billion pounds.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:08.420
If we use the average pound to dollar conversion
rate in 2021 of 1.3757, we’ll see that this

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:14.489
money printing correlates to $1.231 trillion
which is over 50% higher than what they should’ve

00:04:14.489 --> 00:04:15.709
theoretically printed.

00:04:15.709 --> 00:04:18.780
If you look into the money printing of some
of the other leading nations, you’ll see

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:22.270
that they have fallen into the same trap of
printing more money than the US on a relative

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:23.270
basis.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:27.580
On top of this, many of these countries aren’t
pulling back nearly as aggressively as the

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:28.960
US currently is.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:32.910
And for obvious reasons, this has led to the
US dollar performing quite well in relation

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.199
to these foreign currencies.

00:04:35.199 --> 00:04:40.900
EXTREME UNCERTAINTY:
Aside from fundamental supply and demand factors,

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:44.590
external fears have also influenced the dollar’s
recent parabolic move.

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:49.050
One of the top concerns for most investors
right now is how hawkish the Fed will be over

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:50.680
the next couple of months.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:54.389
Currently, there are rumors floating around
that the Fed will hike rates by 0.75 basis

00:04:54.389 --> 00:04:58.699
point not once but twice in both June and
July.

00:04:58.699 --> 00:05:02.550
Whether this ends up happening or not, the
stock market has already preemptively corrected

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:03.590
for this.

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:06.800
Some would even argue that it has overcorrected
for this.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.111
Regardless of whether the market sell of is
justified or not though, in the end, significant

00:05:11.111 --> 00:05:13.389
amounts of capital have left the stock market.

00:05:13.389 --> 00:05:17.300
By now, I’m sure most of you guys have seen
my videos on how several companies have been

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:20.300
shedding hundreds of billions of dollars in
market cap.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:24.620
And while a lot of this value just evaporates
away, the primary place the remaining capital

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:29.740
is going is cash which is significantly driving
up the demand for US dollars.

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:33.570
Something else to note is that it’s only
the stock market that has acted preemptively,

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:36.060
the real estate market is yet to react.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:40.030
If the rumors regarding the fed end up coming
true, the Fed would have raised interest rates

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:43.110
by 2% within a matter of 6 months.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:47.830
Theoretically, home prices should drop 10%
for every 1% interest rates go up.

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:52.130
And given that home prices have just started
to level off, this could mean that they have

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:54.520
a rough 6 to 12 months ahead.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:59.419
And once again were does all the cash leaving
the real estate market go, it goes to cash

00:05:59.419 --> 00:06:02.229
which further increases demand for the dollar
in the short term.

00:06:02.229 --> 00:06:06.000
Also, it’s not just people pulling back
on investments either.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.560
Some of the largest companies in the world
will substantially pull back as well.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:14.040
Now of course, Elon is out there buying Twitter,
but he’s an anomaly.

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:16.639
Instead, take a look at someone like Amazon.

00:06:16.639 --> 00:06:21.270
Amazon just posted their first quarterly loss
in 7 years due to losses from Rivian, increasing

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:23.440
supply chain costs, and slowing revenue growth.

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:28.030
Do you really think Amazon is going to go
out and make big acquisitions anytime soon?

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:29.039
Probably not.

00:06:29.039 --> 00:06:32.630
The more likely scenario is that they’re
going to hoard cash and prepare for a market

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:33.630
downturn.

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:37.610
Similarly, Apple is warning investors that
sales could drop by as much as $8 billion

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:38.759
within the coming months.

00:06:38.759 --> 00:06:43.479
Pair this with rumors that the iPhone 14 will
be even more underwhelming than usual, and

00:06:43.479 --> 00:06:47.250
I don’t its very likely that Apple will
be making any big moves anytime soon.

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:49.690
And that’s just Apple and Amazon.

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:54.100
I don’t even think I need to discuss the
states of PayPal, Netflix, and Facebook.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:58.759
Now, none of this is to say that a market
crash is even around the corner in the first

00:06:58.759 --> 00:06:59.759
place.

00:06:59.759 --> 00:07:02.980
After all, crashes don’t happen when everyone
is expecting it.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:07.470
But, the key is that everyone from retail
investors and small businesses to hedge funds

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:11.389
and mega cap companies are expecting it and
consequently preparing for it.

00:07:11.389 --> 00:07:15.969
And all of this uncertainty is giving the
US dollar significant buying pressure.

00:07:15.969 --> 00:07:21.379
NO INFLATION HEDGING:
During times of high inflation, one of first

00:07:21.379 --> 00:07:25.750
things investors do is move a significant
portion of their wealth in inflation hedges.

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:29.160
Historically, the most popular inflation hedge
has been gold.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:34.360
For example, in the 1970s, when the US was
experiencing double digit inflation rates,

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:36.759
the price of gold went vertical.

00:07:36.759 --> 00:07:43.580
Between 1976 and 1980, the price of gold went
from $100 per ounce to $875 per ounce.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:49.280
Today, inflation is once again almost in double
digit territory; however, people haven’t

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:51.509
really been flocking towards gold.

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:54.999
In fact, gold hasn’t really moved since
July of 2020.

00:07:54.999 --> 00:07:58.259
Not to mention, it’s not like this are new
levels for gold either.

00:07:58.259 --> 00:08:02.759
In fact, gold reached these same levels way
back in 2011.

00:08:02.759 --> 00:08:05.860
If we take a look at silver, the story is
even bleaker.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:13.620
Between 1976 and 1980, silver grew over 10x
from $3.80 per ounce to $48.34 per ounce.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:18.229
Today, however, like Gold, Silver hasn’t
moved since July of 2020.

00:08:18.229 --> 00:08:22.639
What makes silver even worse though is that
it’s literally 50% lower today than it was

00:08:22.639 --> 00:08:24.669
at the peak of 1980.

00:08:24.669 --> 00:08:28.849
Of course, many Silver bulls would suggest
that this is precisely why Silver is such

00:08:28.849 --> 00:08:32.159
a good investment and that it’s extremely
undervalued currently.

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:37.050
Now, they could be absolustely right and Silver
may be on the cusp of a historic rally, but

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:41.789
as of right now, Silver nor Gold has really
done much meaning that very little cash has

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:43.630
flowed into these inflation hedges.

00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:47.630
It’s not just historic inflation hedges
that aren’t getting any love either.

00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:50.140
The best example of this is Bitcoin.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:55.050
Many Bitcoin bulls suggest that that Bitcoin
is the new gold and that its the perfect inflation

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:58.230
hedge given that it’s supply is capped at
21 million coins.

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:02.970
Ethereum bulls would argue that Ether is an
even greater inflation hedge than Bitcoin

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:06.870
given that Ether is actually headed towards
becoming a deflationary cryptocurrency.

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:11.440
Yet, despite all this, neither Bitcoin nor
Ethereum are rallying substantially.

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:14.459
In fact, they’re both down nearly 50%.

00:09:14.459 --> 00:09:19.540
Now, I gotta say, even though Bitcoin and
Ethereum are by no means making new all time

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:23.389
highs, they’ve actually been holding up
extremely well given that they’re down less

00:09:23.389 --> 00:09:24.850
than companies like Netflix and PayPal.

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:29.370
With that being said though, we have yet to
see any meaningful move towards inflation

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:33.050
hedges meaning that all the money people are
cashing out of the stock market and the bond

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:35.279
market is just staying cash.

00:09:35.279 --> 00:09:39.060
And this is keeping demand for the dollar
quite high.

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:44.180
STATE OF THE DOLLAR:
At the end of the day, the main takeaway from

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:48.940
all of this is that despite all of the Fed’s
quantitative easing, the dollar is actually

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:51.709
still holding up extremely strong from a global
perspective.

00:09:51.709 --> 00:09:56.160
Now, this has nothing to do with inflation
or prices.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:00.620
After all, if the US has a 10% inflation rate
while the rest of the world has a 15% inflation

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:04.380
rate, the dollar would still be performing
extremely well in relation.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:08.080
The main factors driving forward the dollar
right now is enormous amounts of money printing

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:13.000
from other countries, extreme uncertainty
in the market regarding the fed, and very

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:14.589
little inflation hedging take place.

00:10:14.589 --> 00:10:18.180
Looking forward, whether this momentum will
continue or not comes down to the Feds stance

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:22.470
over the next couple months and whether inflation
starts to cool down substantially.

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:26.649
If inflation cools down and the Fed backs
off, then it’s very possible that we enter

00:10:26.649 --> 00:10:29.000
a risk on market once again.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:32.250
And during a risk on market, people don’t
hold cash.

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:36.600
Rather, they invest it all into assets which
will drive down demand for the dollar.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:40.490
This could cause the DXY to stagnate or even
start trending down.

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:44.639
On the other hand, if inflation continues
to get worse and the Fed becomes more hawkish,

00:10:44.639 --> 00:10:48.940
the market might become even more risk off
causing the dollar to reach levels that it

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:52.690
hasn’t seen in decades, but we’ll just
have to wait and see.

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:55.560
Do you guys think the DXY is going up or down?

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:57.190
Comment that down below.

00:10:57.190 --> 00:11:01.000
Also, drop a like if you’re hoping that
inflation cools off sooner rather than later.

